            <ArticleTitle>All that wheezes is not asthma.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2013-202369</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr2013202369</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A young woman was admitted with respiratory failure. Prior to her admission, she had been treated for clinical bronchial asthma for about 2 years for recurrent wheezing. Endotracheal intubation was difficult. A fibroptic bronchoscopy, while the patient was ventilated, revealed a central tracheal tumour compromising &gt;90% of the tracheal lumen. The tumour was cored out during rigid bronchoscopy while ventilating the patient through tracheostomy. Histopathology of the tumour was suggestive of adenoid cystic carcinoma.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
